# What a story can do

This page is a reference. Each section demonstrates one capability, and
the last one demonstrates a limit. View source on the rendered HTML to
see exactly what reached the browser — nothing here is rewritten by
frontflow.

Two different moments matter on this page:

- **Render time** — when someone ran `frontflow story render`. Python and
  R cells run here, once, and their output is baked into the HTML.
- **View time** — when you opened this page. Only JavaScript runs here.

## 1. Ordinary Markdown

Everything Markdown does, including GFM tables:

| Region | Submissions | Share |
|--------|------------:|------:|
| East   |           6 |   43% |
| North  |           4 |   29% |
| West   |           2 |   14% |
| South  |           2 |   14% |

## 2. Python, executed at render time

A cell runs when the page is built. The source is shown by default:

```{python}
import datetime
print("rendered at", datetime.datetime.now().strftime("%H:%M:%S"))
```

Reload this page: the time above never changes, because it is not being
computed. That is the whole point of pre-rendering — the reader does not
pay for the report.

`#| echo: false` hides the source and shows only the output:

```{python}
#| echo: false
sales = {"East": 6, "North": 4, "West": 2, "South": 2}
total = sum(sales.values())
for region, n in sorted(sales.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]):
    print(f"{region:<6} {'#' * (n * 3):<20} {n:>2}  ({n / total:.0%})")
```

`#| eval: false` does the opposite — shows the code without running it,
for when you want to talk about code rather than execute it.

## 3. Your own CSS

A `{html}` cell passes through verbatim, so a `<style>` block is yours.
frontflow's own stylesheet never reaches this page: it is a separate
document, so there is nothing to collide with.

```{html}
<style>
  .swatch {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(7rem, 1fr));
    gap: .75rem; margin: 1rem 0;
  }
  .swatch div {
    padding: 1rem .75rem; border-radius: .5rem; color: #fff;
    font: 600 .8125rem ui-monospace, monospace;
  }
</style>
<div class="swatch">
  <div style="background:#4c1d95">rebecca</div>
  <div style="background:#065f46">forest</div>
  <div style="background:#7c2d12">rust</div>
  <div style="background:#1e3a8a">navy</div>
</div>
```

## 4. Your own JavaScript

Scripts run at view time, in the reader's browser. This one writes the
current time, so unlike the Python above it *does* change on reload —
which is the clearest way to see the two moments apart.

```{html}
<p>Baked by Python at render time, above. Written by JavaScript just now:
  <strong id="live">script did not run</strong>
</p>
<script>
  document.getElementById("live").textContent =
    new Date().toTimeString().slice(0, 8);
</script>
```

## 5. Loading a library

Nothing in the response restricts `script-src`, so a story can pull a
library from a CDN and use it. This is also how you would enable
Observable JS: xmd emits an `ojs` cell as inert markup carrying its own
source, and an Observable runtime you load yourself is what claims it.

```{ojs}
answer = 40 + 2
```

frontflow bundles no Observable runtime, so that cell is inert as it
stands. Here is what actually arrived in the page, read back out of the
DOM — the source is intact and waiting for an interpreter:

```{html}
<pre id="ojs-probe" style="font-size:.8125rem">reading…</pre>
<script>
  var cells = document.querySelectorAll(".ojs-cell");
  var lines = ["ojs cells found: " + cells.length];
  cells.forEach(function (cell, i) {
    var src = cell.querySelector(".ojs-source");
    lines.push("  [" + i + "] data-ojs present: " + cell.hasAttribute("data-ojs"));
    lines.push("  [" + i + "] source hidden:    " + (src && src.hasAttribute("hidden")));
    lines.push("  [" + i + "] source text:      " + (src && src.textContent));
  });
  document.getElementById("ojs-probe").textContent = lines.join("\n");
</script>
```

## 6. The limit: this page is isolated

The document is served with a `sandbox` directive that grants
`allow-scripts` but deliberately **not** `allow-same-origin`, so it runs
on an opaque origin. It cannot read frontflow's cookies, its storage, or
the page framing it — and neither can anything a story prints, which is
what makes it safe to skip sanitising.

The probe below tries each of those and reports what happened. Every
line should say blocked:

```{html}
<pre id="iso" style="font-size:.8125rem">probing…</pre>
<script>
  function probe(label, fn) {
    try {
      var v = fn();
      return label + ": " + (v === "" || v == null
        ? "empty (blocked)"
        : "READABLE -> " + String(v).slice(0, 40));
    } catch (e) {
      return label + ": blocked (" + e.name + ")";
    }
  }
  document.getElementById("iso").textContent = [
    probe("origin              ", function () { return document.origin; }),
    probe("document.cookie     ", function () { return document.cookie; }),
    probe("localStorage        ", function () { return localStorage.length; }),
    probe("parent.document.title", function () { return parent.document.title; }),
    probe("parent.location.href", function () { return parent.location.href; }),
    "",
    "postMessage to parent still works — it is how this",
    "page reports its height so the panel can size to it."
  ].join("\n");
</script>
```

## 7. Taking over the whole document

If you want no shell at all, emit `<!doctype html>` from a `{html}` cell
as the first thing in the file. frontflow detects it and serves your
document byte for byte: no wrapper, no default styles, and no height
reporter — so declare `min_height` on the panel in that case.

## Rendering this

```bash
frontflow story render ./workflows
frontflow story render ./workflows --check   # CI: fails if stale
```

Edit this file without re-rendering and the workspace will tell you the
page was built from an older source, rather than quietly serving stale
output or re-running the report while you wait.
